21:46:15 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Grades are a measure of academic performance in a particular setting, not a direct measure of intelligence. They reflect ability to some extent, but also attendance, time available, sleep, prior preparation, course difficulty, assignment completion, teacher grading practices, test anxiety, family responsibilities, coaching schedules, and whether a student is being placed into—or chooses—more or less demanding courses. A student can be capable of stronger academic work while earning lower grades because practice, travel, fatigue, or missed instruction undermines performance. Likewise, taking easier classes can reflect school placement, eligibility requirements, counselor advice, or a strategic choice to manage a demanding athletic schedule. It does not by itself establish lower underlying ability. If a group consistently has lower grades in a given context, that is worth investigating. But the conclusion should be about the observed academic outcomes and the conditions producing them—not a blanket claim that every member is less intelligent.
